.venv/
__pycache__/
*.pyc
.pytest_cache/
logs/
.superpowers/
data/
replays/
data/replay_buffer/
.claude/worktrees/
_dbg/
internal_notes/
.planning/

# Working notes: kept on disk, never shared. This is a PUBLIC repo, and the
# specs/plans under here quote the author directly and name his other projects
# (user, 2026-07-27, having made the same call in task_tracker and
# claude-console the same day: "we should just .gitignore it, but should keep
# it locally, for reference. It's useful, just shouldn't be shared").
# 47 of these files were already pushed before this rule existed and remain
# reachable in git history; removing THAT needs a history rewrite.
docs/superpowers/

# Same reasoning as docs/superpowers/ above: the task tracker's notes quote the
# author and name his other projects. Listed HERE, in the tracked .gitignore,
# rather than relying on the self-ignoring `.tasks/.gitignore` (a file
# containing `*`, which therefore ignores itself and is untracked). That worked
# in this checkout and nowhere else — a fresh clone or a new git worktree has
# no such file, so `.tasks/` was NOT ignored there and
# tests/test_docs_links_resolve.py::test_every_private_dir_is_actually_ignored
# failed the moment this branch was built in a worktree (2026-07-28). A guard
# that depends on an untracked file is the exact "must resolve in a fresh
# clone" failure that test exists to catch.
.tasks/

# bundled package data (rank-standards seed) must be tracked despite the data/ rule above
!src/sm64_events/data/
!src/sm64_events/data/**

# desktop build + runtime state
/dist/
/build/
*.spec
/server.pid
/window.json
